  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Holistic programme of HIV prevention and care in El Salvador</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In El Salvador, estimates of people living with HIV and AIDS vary substantially. Many people do not know that they carry the virus, or if they do know, they often try to keep it secret for fear of social discrimination. By June 2007 there were 19,153 cases of HIV positive people registered with the state health authorities. Of these, about half have AIDS, which indicates significant levels of subregistration. Young people and women make up a growing proportion of cases and the estimated 4-5 new infections per day in El Salvador are concentrated amongst poor and marginalised people: workers, housewives and youth.  

Many factors have contributed to the spread of HIV in El Salvador, including poverty, unemployment and lack of information. CONTRASIDA considers that the single greatest contributing factor is the machista culture prevalent in the country that encourages men to have multiple relationships and undermines the status of women.

Those organisations working in the field of HIV are unable to provide sufficient coverage for the size of the population. The Global Fund is focused on treatment for people living with HIV and AIDS (PLWHA) and support for preventive education is focused on men having sex with men (MSM), commercial sex workers and adolescents. CONTRASIDA insists that general population is at risk and behavioral change amongst all people should be encouraged. As such this project promotes a holistic approach to HIV prevention and care, working with youth theatre groups, parishes, diocesan groups, schools and communities to raise awareness of the issues and encourage solidarity with PLWHAs and providing holistic care and treatment for those living with the virus.</narrative>
  </description>
